UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 1: Using space, props and costumes', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nIn a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and write the reason for each choice. Perform the scene using things you already have at home and keep walkways clear.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station. Draw a stage plan showing where actors stand, add two props and one costume piece, and write the reason for each choice. Perform the scene using things you alre Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using the stage space on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating the stage space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with the stage space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about the stage space with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for the stage space. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for the stage space. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson."],"question":"In a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for the stage space; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane.","Careful use of a prop and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a prop and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a prop and explain why it matters only, without linking it to the stage space.","Apply the stage space to new material and name the a prop and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene."],"question":"Which response best applies the stage space with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on the stage space. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for the stage space."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for the stage space?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose a scene such as a bakery, a ship or a space station.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.","Name a prop and explain why it matters only, with no linked example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies the stage space to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about the stage space with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating the stage space on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using the stage space on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for the stage space. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with the stage space, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w14-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise the stage space.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use the stage space with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a prop and explain why it matters.","I can practise a costume independently on a new prompt.","I can notice a level needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who.","options":["Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about the stage space with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nTRY FIRST\nIn a scene about a shy bear and a proud lion, say where each stands on stage, what level each uses and what simple prop or costume each might have.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.","explain":"Drama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Level and space on stage.","caption":"Place actors with purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">king on a box, servant kneels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lion front, bear back</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience know Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience know Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about the stage space with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"A prop and a costume.","caption":"Objects tell the story.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Prop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">torch in a dark cave</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Costume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">paper crown for a king</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about the stage space with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Here is a more helpful example: Example: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Lion stands in the centre front, tall, with a yellow cardboard mane. Bear stays at the back, low and curled, holding a small basket.'' The lion''s strong position and high level show pride, and the bear''s low and back position shows shyness.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that more props and costumes make a better scene. Too many props can distract and be unsafe, and a single well-chosen object can say more than lots of clutter. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about the stage space with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDrama uses space, props and costumes to tell the audience where and who. The stage is the acting area, and actors use space by choosing where to stand: near the front can feel powerful and at the back feels distant. A prop is an object used in a scene, such as a lantern or a basket. A costume is clothing that shows the character, such as a cape for a king. Levels, such as standing, kneeling and sitting, make the scene interesting to look at. Keep walkways clear and use only safe props. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 14, Lesson 1: Using space, props and costumes","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Role, character and storytelling","week":14,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: In a scene set in a cave, Priya turns off a light, crouches low and holds a torch (a prop) so the audience knows it is dark. The king wears a paper crown (a costume) and stands on a box (a higher level) to look important. The servant kneels in front. Space, props and costumes tell the story without any explanation.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Level and space on stage.","caption":"Place actors with purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Level</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">king on a box, servant kneels</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lion front, bear back</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A prop and a costume.","caption":"Objects tell the story.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Prop</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">torch in a dark cave</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Costume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">paper crown for a king</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-arts-y3-w14-l1' AND family_id IS NULL;
